WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.732 --> 00:00:13.418
The train from Bombay to Calcutta set out punctually at 8:00 p.m.

2
00:00:14.878 --> 00:00:16.259
Among the passengers, to Mr.

3
00:00:16.299 --> 00:00:21.401
Fogg's delight, was Sir Francis Cromarty, a fellow whist player from the Mongolia.

4
00:00:22.702 --> 00:00:26.764
Sir Francis had made India his home and was returning after a trip to England.

5
00:00:27.828 --> 00:00:29.268
He asked to sit with Mr.

6
00:00:29.288 --> 00:00:31.829
Fogg, and they quickly fell into friendly conversation.

7
00:00:33.290 --> 00:00:34.870
Sir Francis had never seen Mr.

8
00:00:34.910 --> 00:00:39.791
Fogg take in the sights during the boat voyage, and he noticed that on the train his companion

9
00:00:39.831 --> 00:00:40.552
was no different.

10
00:00:42.132 --> 00:00:45.473
Phileas Fogg talked with Sir Francis, ignoring Passepartout.

11
00:00:46.573 --> 00:00:49.914
He never looked out the window and didn't comment on the scenery at all.

12
00:00:49.934 --> 00:00:54.516
"Does the man have any sense of the beauties of nature?"

13
00:00:54.856 --> 00:00:55.396
he wondered.

14
00:00:57.000 --> 00:00:58.120
On the Mongolia Mr.

15
00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:01.841
Fogg had explained to Sir Francis the reason for his trip around the world.

16
00:01:03.202 --> 00:01:06.763
Sir Francis thought the extravagant bet was a pointless eccentricity.

17
00:01:06.783 --> 00:01:09.104
"It lacks common sense!"

18
00:01:09.544 --> 00:01:09.944
he thought.

19
00:01:09.964 --> 00:01:16.266
"The way this strange gentleman is going on, he will leave this world without having done anything good

20
00:01:16.326 --> 00:01:18.186
for himself or for anyone else."

21
00:01:19.867 --> 00:01:20.747
He watched Mr.

22
00:01:20.787 --> 00:01:24.188
Fogg enter their departure from Bombay in his travel journal.

23
00:01:25.904 --> 00:01:27.025
"Some years ago, Mr.

24
00:01:27.065 --> 00:01:32.148
Fogg," said Sir Francis, "you would have met a delay at this point, which probably would have lost

25
00:01:32.188 --> 00:01:32.929
you your wager."

26
00:01:32.949 --> 00:01:35.771
"How so, Sir Francis?"

27
00:01:36.211 --> 00:01:36.811
asked Mr.

28
00:01:36.851 --> 00:01:43.216
Fogg. "The railway stopped at the base of these mountains, which the passengers were obliged to cross in

29
00:01:43.276 --> 00:01:45.157
hand-carried carts, called palanquins.

30
00:01:46.438 --> 00:01:49.620
Then they would board another train in Kandallah, on the other side."

31
00:01:49.640 --> 00:01:54.884
"Such a delay would not have thrown off my plans in the least," replied Mr.

32
00:01:54.924 --> 00:01:59.049
Fogg. "I have foreseen the likelihood of certain obstacles."

33
00:01:59.069 --> 00:02:01.651
"But, Mr.

34
00:02:01.691 --> 00:02:07.354
Fogg," continued Sir Francis, "you do run the risk of having some difficulty about your servant's adventure at

35
00:02:07.374 --> 00:02:07.934
the pagoda."

36
00:02:09.395 --> 00:02:14.898
Passepartout, his feet wrapped in a blanket, was sound asleep, with no idea that the men were talking

37
00:02:14.938 --> 00:02:15.339
about him.

38
00:02:15.359 --> 00:02:19.281
"The government is very severe about that kind of offense.

39
00:02:21.282 --> 00:02:24.904
It takes particular care that the religious customs of the Indians are respected.

40
00:02:25.972 --> 00:02:30.674
If your servant were caught "Very well, Sir Francis," replied Mr.

41
00:02:30.714 --> 00:02:37.317
Fogg. "If Passepartout had been caught, he would have been condemned and punished, and then he would have

42
00:02:37.357 --> 00:02:38.718
quietly returned to Europe.

43
00:02:39.758 --> 00:02:44.300
It wouldn't have stopped me from completing my journey, nor would it have delayed me in the least!"

44
00:02:45.981 --> 00:02:48.122
After this exchange the men fell silent.

45
00:02:49.843 --> 00:02:52.004
During the night the train left the mountains behind.

46
00:02:54.828 --> 00:02:59.609
Passepartout woke the next morning, and when he looked out his window, he saw the splendor of the

47
00:02:59.689 --> 00:03:01.310
great Indian subcontinent.

48
00:03:03.050 --> 00:03:07.712
The train passed cotton, coffee, nutmeg, clove, and pepper plantations.

49
00:03:09.352 --> 00:03:12.053
The fertile land was watered by many rivers and streams.

50
00:03:14.013 --> 00:03:18.815
He saw picturesque low houses and marvelous temples surrounded by groves of palm trees.

51
00:03:19.875 --> 00:03:23.556
Further on, the train entered a vast jungle that extended to the horizon.

52
00:03:25.452 --> 00:03:29.816
The jungle was inhabited by snakes and tigers, which fled at the noise of the train.

53
00:03:31.878 --> 00:03:34.741
Elephants with pensive eyes gazed at the train as it passed.

54
00:03:36.923 --> 00:03:42.869
At half past 12, the train stopped at Burhampoor, where Passepartout was able to purchase some Indian slippers

55
00:03:43.069 --> 00:03:44.330
ornamented with false pearls.

56
00:03:46.552 --> 00:03:52.560
Back on the train, the sights from the car window entranced Passepartout.

57
00:03:52.580 --> 00:03:57.184
His old vagabond nature was coming back to him, and he recalled all the fantastic ideas of his

58
00:03:57.204 --> 00:04:04.470
youth. He monitored the train's progress anxiously, getting impatient whenever it slowed or paused.

59
00:04:06.631 --> 00:04:10.815
At eight o'clock the train stopped in the middle of a clearing where there were several huts and

60
00:04:10.875 --> 00:04:11.755
workmen's cabins.

61
00:04:13.557 --> 00:04:17.580
The conductor passed through the cars and shouted, "Passengers will get out here!"

62
00:04:19.148 --> 00:04:24.290
Phileas Fogg looked at Sir Francis for an explanation, but Sir Francis had no idea what was going

63
00:04:24.450 --> 00:04:30.353
on. Passepartout, also surprised, rushed out of the car to see what the problem was.

64
00:04:31.594 --> 00:04:34.855
He speedily returned, crying, "Monsieur, no more railway!

65
00:04:34.955 --> 00:04:36.096
No more railway!"

66
00:04:36.116 --> 00:04:38.177
"What do you mean?"

67
00:04:38.617 --> 00:04:39.677
asked Sir Francis.

68
00:04:39.697 --> 00:04:45.540
"I mean to say that the train isn't going on," said Passepartout, breathless.

69
00:04:47.172 --> 00:04:49.554
Sir Francis immediately stepped out and, with Mr.

70
00:04:49.594 --> 00:04:52.737
Fogg following calmly behind, walked over to the conductor.

71
00:04:52.757 --> 00:04:55.460
"Where are we?"

72
00:04:55.860 --> 00:04:56.861
asked Sir Francis.

73
00:04:56.881 --> 00:05:00.945
"At the village of Kholby," replied the conductor.

74
00:05:00.965 --> 00:05:03.367
"Do we stop here?"

75
00:05:03.787 --> 00:05:04.808
asked Sir Francis.

76
00:05:04.828 --> 00:05:07.731
"Certainly," said the conductor.

77
00:05:07.751 --> 00:05:09.612
"The railway isn't finished."

78
00:05:09.632 --> 00:05:12.055
"What! Not finished?"

79
00:05:12.415 --> 00:05:13.336
cried Sir Francis.

80
00:05:14.604 --> 00:05:19.168
"There are still fifty miles of track to be laid from here to Allahabad," said the conductor.

81
00:05:19.188 --> 00:05:25.433
"But you sell tickets from Bombay to Calcutta," said Sir Francis with exasperation.

82
00:05:25.453 --> 00:05:28.555
"Yes," said the conductor.

83
00:05:28.575 --> 00:05:34.360
"But the passengers know they must find their own transport from Kholby to Allahabad."

84
00:05:36.161 --> 00:05:37.482
Sir Francis was furious.

85
00:05:38.503 --> 00:05:41.245
Passepartout was angry enough to knock the conductor down.

86
00:05:42.867 --> 00:05:44.008
They both turned to Mr.

87
00:05:44.048 --> 00:05:46.233
Fogg, who regarded them as calmly as ever.

88
00:05:50.737 --> 00:05:55.180
As I sat on that train from Bombay to Calcutta, I never stopped looking out the window, except

89
00:05:55.200 --> 00:05:56.161
to sleep—ha-ha!

90
00:05:57.422 --> 00:06:02.286
India is such a lush green country, and I saw so many plantations growing all sorts of crops.

91
00:06:03.687 --> 00:06:08.631
By colonizing India and setting up railways, the British have been able to turn India into a center

92
00:06:08.691 --> 00:06:09.872
of commercial agriculture.

93
00:06:11.360 --> 00:06:15.382
The three most important crops in India are coffee, indigo, and tea.

94
00:06:16.502 --> 00:06:17.943
Tea is especially important.

95
00:06:18.964 --> 00:06:21.625
There are over three- hundred tea plantations in India!

96
00:06:22.145 --> 00:06:24.266
They produce three-thousand tons of the stuff!

97
00:06:25.487 --> 00:06:26.107
It's a shame though.

98
00:06:26.727 --> 00:06:31.369
Despite all that rich, fertile land and all those crops, many Indians are starving to death.

99
00:06:32.730 --> 00:06:35.951
The crops are exported by Great Britain and sold overseas for profit.
